<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Severn</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Severn is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, about 354 kilometres long, rising on Plynlimon in mid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and flowing through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shropshire">Shropshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcestershire">Worcestershire</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bristol">Bristol</a> Channel. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its valley formed a frontier zone between the Welsh kingdoms and Mercia. Towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a> grew at its crossings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-severn.jpg" alt="River Severn" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Chrisbayley at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SevernFromCastleCB.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
